 Capabilities
 ------------
 - Scalar or multi-dimensional endogenous treatment
-- Multiple instruments (over-identified models handled via GMM-style
-  quadratic form in ``b``)
+- Multiple instruments. By default (``instrument_form='projection'``) they
+  are collapsed to the least-squares projection of ``D`` on ``[X, Z]``, the
+  "simple procedure" of Chernozhukov & Hansen (Section 3.1) and what the R
+  package ``IVQR`` does; ``instrument_form='raw'`` keeps every instrument
+  and minimises the identity-weighted ``||b(alpha)||^2``.
 - Grid-search + local refinement over α (scalar) or BFGS over α
   (multi-dim)
-- Bootstrap standard errors for α̂(τ) (pairs bootstrap)
+- Analytic (kernel sandwich) standard errors for ``(alpha, beta)`` when the
+  auxiliary regression is exactly identified; pairs-bootstrap SEs on request
 - Multiple quantiles in one call

 Reference behaviour
 -------------------
-With one endogenous regressor and one instrument the estimate is the root
-of the instrument's coefficient in the auxiliary quantile regression; it
-reproduces that root computed with R ``quantreg::rq``
-(tests/reference_parity/test_rd_iv_R_parity.py). With more instruments
-than endogenous regressors the criterion here weights ``b(alpha)`` by the
-identity, whereas Chernozhukov & Hansen weight it by the inverse of its
-estimated covariance, so the over-identified estimates are a different
-(consistent) member of the family. (An earlier version of this docstring
-claimed agreement with Stata ``ivqreg2`` and "R's ``quantreg::ivqreg``";
-``ivqreg2`` implements the Machado-Santos Silva moments estimator and
-``quantreg`` has no ``ivqreg``.)
+The point estimate is the root of the instrument coefficient ``b(alpha)``
+in the auxiliary quantile regression of ``Y - D alpha`` on ``[X, D_hat]``
+(``D_hat`` the first-stage projection). It is held against R ``IVQR`` 0.1.0
+(``ivqr``, Yu-Chang Chen, GitHub ``yuchang0321/IVQR``) and Stata 18's
+official ``ivqregress iqr`` on a just- and an over-identified design,
+estimate and every SE, in ``tests/reference_parity/test_r2_rdiv_parity.py``.
+Both references return the minimiser of a Wald criterion over a grid; with
+``dim(b) = dim(alpha)`` that criterion is zero exactly at the root, so the
+fixtures pin the root (IVQR: one-point grid at the root; Stata: a symmetric
+odd grid centred on it). The analytic SE is the Chernozhukov-Hansen
+sandwich ``V = J^{-1} S J^{-T} / n``, ``S = tau (1 - tau) Psi'Psi / n``,
+``J = (n h)^{-1} sum K(e/h) Psi (D, X)'``, ``Psi = [D_hat, X]`` (invariant
+to replacing ``D_hat`` by ``Z`` in the just-identified case). Defaults
+follow Stata ``ivqregress`` (Epanechnikov kernel, Silverman bandwidth with
+Stata's quartiles); ``vce_kernel='uniform', vce_bwidth='ivqr'`` gives R
+``IVQR::ivqr.vc`` (``h = 1.364 (2 sqrt(pi))^(-1/5) sd(e) n^(-1/5)``).
+Stata's default *point estimate* is the best of 30 adaptive grid points, so
+it differs from the root by up to half a grid step.
+
+Through StatsPAI 1.28.0 every instrument entered the criterion with
+identity weight. Chernozhukov & Hansen allow any positive-definite weight,
+but that choice is not invariant to the units of the instruments (rescaling
+one instrument by 50 moved the median estimate by 1%) and matches no
+reference implementation; it remains available as ``instrument_form='raw'``
+(with bootstrap SEs only). An earlier docstring also claimed agreement with
+Stata ``ivqreg2`` (the Machado-Santos Silva moments estimator, a different
+estimator) and "R's ``quantreg::ivqreg``" (which does not exist).

